 This install applies to "4D Sports Driving" © 1992 Mindscape

 This install requires WHDLoad 17.0 or newer to be in the path. It can be found
 in the WHDLoad package.

 The installed program requires 0.5 MB ChipMem and 0.5 MB OtherMem.
 It can be quit by pressing num '*' (key can be changed with Tooltype "QuitKey=").

 The game requires an installed A500 Kickstart 1.3 image.
 The kickstart image must be located in the directory "Devs:Kickstarts" and
 must be named "kick34005.A500". The image can be extracted from a A500 with
 tools like 'GrabKick' or similar which can be found on aminet.
 If the image is of wrong version or altered in any way WHDLoad will quit with
 an appropriate requester.
 Additionally the file "kick34005.A500.RTB" must be installed in the same
 directory as the kickstart image. It can be found in the archive 
 "util/boot/skick346.lha" on aminet.


 If you have problems with this installation please try and get a newer
 version of this package from the WHDLoad WWW-Page. If that doesn't help try
 some Tooltypes like NOCACHE, NOAUTOVEC, NOVBRMOVE, NOMMU, PAL or NTSC. If
 that also fails, please inform the author via the Bug-Report-Form which
 can be accessed on the WHDLoad WWW-Page.

 For the full WHDLoad package, other installs and updates refer to:
	http://www.whdload.de/
 or	aminet:dev/misc/whdload#?
 and	aminet:game/patch/

 Current production source, P1 timing correction validated (11.10.2026):
 - the default build now uses an independent display-field clock and input
   queue for Normal (10 Hz) and A2500 (20 Hz), without A4000 physics or fixtures
 - CUSTOM3=1 restores original simulation timing and original timing-hook
   bytes; CUSTOM2 continues to control rendering optimizations separately
 - PAL/NTSC is detected from the beam; the clock uses a 50/60 phase accumulator
 - 30-second emulator checks: Normal 10.033 steps/s on A500 FULL/FAST;
   A2500 20.002 on PAL 020 and 19.997 on NTSC 020. Race clock ratios are
   1.00329, 1.00009 and 0.99987 respectively; all meet the +/-0.5% timing gate
 - this does not deliver the predicted A500 FAST frame-rate gain: matched
   10-second runs measured 7.697 FPS with legacy timing and 7.597 corrected,
   while simulation speed improved from 8.296 to 9.995 steps/s
 - native 10/20 Hz replay loading and playback pass. All 1120 captured
   simulation-state bytes match legacy timing at replay step 200
 - 120-second moving stock-track drives on A500 FULL/FAST and 020 FULL
   have no queue overflow. Combined crash/restart/finish checks also pass
   over 177.60 / 176.72 / 182.52 seconds respectively; those functional
   runs use a scenery-cleared track and include a long idle prelude
 - P1 timing and compatibility acceptance passes. The A500 FAST target of
   9.5-10 FPS belongs to P2; no P2 rendering work has been started
 - this source build has not replaced the previously distributed archive

 Separate A4000 SIM_RUNTIME source status (11.10.2026):
 - replay format 2, physics ABI9, stores the chosen opponent route so native
   resource reloads cannot silently change it. Older format-1 A4000 files
   are rejected; Normal/A2500 replay formats are unchanged
 - 38 codec checks pass. First/repeated FULL, FAST and rewind playback each
   match all 1510 state bytes at 250 checkpoints in the regression replay
 - that regression file was reconstructed from an existing recording and its
   saved route. Native format-2 disk save/load is NOT yet verified: the last
   UI attempt left an empty file and did not reach the replay writer
 - the original opponent route builder's out-of-resource lookup remains;
   deterministic fresh A4000 route setup is still open
 - jump/landing handling and broader lifecycle, latency/capacity and release
   acceptance remain incomplete. This is an experimental preview
 - the previously distributed testing archive has not been rebuilt with
   this source change; it still contains the earlier format-1 runtime

 A4000 testing archive v1.3b:
 - this pack includes the playable SIM_RUNTIME preview, without keyboard
   automation, synthetic test fixtures or startup self-tests
 - launch the existing game icon; use Return to advance the opening screens
 - open Ctrl-G and select A4000 for 50 Hz physics; PAL is required
 - the simulation change takes effect on the NEXT race. Close the dialog,
   acknowledge the notice, then choose Re-Start Driving in the replay menu
 - set graphics detail to maximum in the same dialog; cursor keys drive
 - CUSTOM3=0 enables independent simulation timing (the default in this pack)
 - CUSTOM3=1 restores legacy timing and the original Normal/A2500 choices;
   saved A4000 preferences are ignored and A4000 replays are rejected safely
 - CUSTOM2=1 enables rendering optimizations; CUSTOM2=0 is the default
   and retains the original rendering routines
 - A4000 replay physics ABI9 retains fractional steering, body position and
   tilt; older development A4000 replays are rejected. Native 10/20 Hz
   replays remain supported
 - use a separate extracted copy for testing. Game data and icons are
   unchanged from the v1.2 pack; only the slave and this ReadMe are replaced
 - the preview has been exercised in FS-UAE with 68020, 2 MB ChipMem and
   4 MB FastMem. Sustained 50 Hz capacity on an A4000 is not yet established
 - known limitation: jump height and landing pitch still differ from A2500
   beyond the current acceptance limits. Broader handling, lifecycle and latency
   validation is unfinished; this is not a final release

 version 1.3b (testing):
 - increase the simulation build stack to 10000 bytes; native opponent
   resource loading could otherwise overwrite DOS memory-management data
   and crash when loading an opponent replay
 - discard stale opening-sequence input when A4000 live recording starts,
   so holding Return through startup cannot extend the replay past its end
 - queue exhaustion exits with a clear WHDLoad message instead of a Guru;
   unread controls are never overwritten
 - prevent A4000 rendering from overwriting the buffer still on screen;
   apply queued input after waiting for the display buffer switch
 - prevent A4000 pitch/roll stabilization from snapping a non-level actual
   angle to zero when only its extrapolated A2500 reference crosses level
 - optional rendering optimizations (CUSTOM2=1), off by default; select the
   rendering option in the WHDLoad startup window, or add CUSTOM2=1
 - suppress redundant blitter interrupts only while the game owns the blitter
   inside primitive drawing; preserve queued work and prior interrupt state
 - faster vertex processing, primitive emission, backface tests, parent-relative
   rotation and primitive depth/header bookkeeping, validated against native
   results
 - faster state clear on 68000; 68010 and later retain the original clear loop
 - CUSTOM2=0 preserves the original bytes at every optimization hook
 - verified in FS-UAE on 68000 (512 KB chip + 1536 KB slow RAM) and 68020
   (2 MB chip + 4 MB fast RAM), including graphics levels, replay, crashes
   and native race finishes; real-hardware testing remains welcome
 - stationary FULL/Normal starting-scene display rate improved about 11.8%
   on both tested machines, measured over 250 sampled PAL fields per option;
   this is a scene-specific result, not a moving-race benchmark

 version 1.2 (21.03.2021) done by JOTD:
 - long delay when ending a race removed (side effect
   of music fix) (issue #0005034)
 - fixed graphical errors on displayed texts
   
 version 1.1 (09.09.2020)
 - music properly fixed (issue #004800)
 - enabled caches everywhere so Vampire and other non MMU
   users can play with caches on (better for 3D games!)
   
 version 1.0 (01.07.2006) done by JOTD:
 - adapted for whdload v16
 - music fixed (not perfect but works better)
 - package fully reworked
 - source code included

 version 1.0 (2001) done by JOTD:
 - initial release
 - SNOOP access fault removed
 - Full use of fastmem and caches for smooth 3D animation
 - Support for track expansion disk
 - password protection removed
 - Docfile included
 - Original quit menu still active
 - Nice icons by Exoticons
